      "response": {
        "segments": [
          {
            "kind": "prose",
            "text": "Workspace-inheritance and project-level sharing are presented as two of the three main archetypes in the research, with explicit pros/cons and a concrete recommendation for TraceLab.",
            "citations": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "prose",
            "text": "**Workspace-inheritance archetype**",
            "citations": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "- Definition: Membership is granted at the container (workspace/teamspace) level, and everything created inside is visible to all members by default.\n- Examples: Notion teamspaces, Miro teams, ClickUp Spaces, Google shared drives.\n- Pros: Very low friction; new projects are instantly collaborative and the access graph is trivial (mirror workspace membership).\n- Cons: Highest blast radius—expanding workspace membership silently expands access to every project; the Linear 2026 incident is cited as evidence that such broad boundaries tend to leak into notifications and cached clients.",
            "citations": [
              "495844eb-ec3b-42d4-81b8-d2d2005c62f6",
              "b4713fac-2d20-45b5-9e51-c52fa6c80d11"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "prose",
            "text": "**Project-level (per-project) sharing archetype**",
            "citations": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "- Definition: Access is granted explicitly per project or artifact; there is no automatic workspace-wide baseline in the pure form.\n- Examples: GitHub repositories, GitLab projects, Dropbox shared folders, Airtable bases are cited as project/artifact-level products in general.\n- Pros (pure per-project rosters option): Smallest possible blast radius per grant; guarantees are simple to state and test.\n- Cons: Onboarding becomes a per-project chore; maintainers accumulate “grant debt,” and vendors often reintroduce broad access via an all-members team, effectively drifting back toward a hybrid model.",
            "citations": [
              "b4713fac-2d20-45b5-9e51-c52fa6c80d11",
              "495844eb-ec3b-42d4-81b8-d2d2005c62f6"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "prose",
            "text": "**Which option the research recommends for TraceLab**",
            "citations": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "The report does not recommend a pure workspace-inheritance or pure per-project model for TraceLab; instead it recommends a **hybrid, project-bounded model** as Option 1.",
            "citations": [
              "b4713fac-2d20-45b5-9e51-c52fa6c80d11"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "- In this recommended model, the workspace provides discovery, naming, and a baseline role, but each project has its own explicit roster that can override the baseline, governed by a clear resolution rule.\n- This is justified as giving isolation by default (explicit project rosters) while preserving discoverability and a path to future federation.\n- The UX guidance is to use a Figma-style share modal that enumerates access sources from broadest to most specific, plus conflict indicators similar to GitHub’s “Mixed roles.”",
            "citations": [
              "b4713fac-2d20-45b5-9e51-c52fa6c80d11"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "prose",
            "text": "**How the recommendation relates to workspace-inheritance vs project-level**",
            "citations": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "- Workspace-inheritance (Option 3) is explicitly labeled the **runner-up** and is recommended only if TraceLab’s projects become short-lived and cross-cutting—i.e., when project participant sets churn so fast that explicit rosters are unreasonable to maintain.\n- The report states that, given the declared “maintainer-and-project” profile for TraceLab, that trigger condition is **not** met, so the hybrid, project-bounded model is a better fit than workspace-inheritance.",
            "citations": [
              "12ede311-cf0d-47ef-9299-6c35846d84fc"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "corpus_claim",
            "text": "In summary, the research views pure workspace-inheritance as simpler but too risky in blast radius, and pure per-project sharing as safest but operationally heavy. For TraceLab’s current use case, it recommends a hybrid with project-bounded rosters (plus an optional admin warning gate) rather than either extreme.",
            "citations": [
              "b4713fac-2d20-45b5-9e51-c52fa6c80d11",
              "12ede311-cf0d-47ef-9299-6c35846d84fc"
            ]
          }
        ],
